{"author":{"id":"e163a01afd61f0ee7cde74b11a7584d479eb3113852f38923c96e8c90e459dcd"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790371553,"nonce":"qnZWbF0mEICKl9ijWEPDBS7zaWQ=","title":"krio-fiber needs an allocation-free no_std context layer with symmetric transfer","message":"krio-fiber is std-only: Fiber boxes its closure, passes values as Box\u003cdyn Any\u003e, names fibers with String, catches panics with catch_unwind, keeps the active fiber in thread-locals, and allocates stacks with mmap. A kernel or bare-metal host (Zura's microkernel components) has no heap, no unwinding and no threads, yet needs exactly the part underneath: lay out a first frame on a stack it provides, and switch between contexts. Fiber also has no symmetric transfer (fiber -\u003e fiber without returning to the host), which a scheduler's direct handoff needs.\n\nWanted:\n- A `raw` module usable with no_std and no alloc: `Context` (a saved stack pointer), `Context::new(stack, entry, arg)` where `entry: extern \"C\" fn(*mut u8) -\u003e !` runs on the first switch in, and `switch(from, to)` saving the running context into `from` and resuming `to` — any context to any other.\n- A `std` feature, on by default, gating Fiber, its stacks and the Task impl; `--no-default-features` builds for aarch64-unknown-none and x86_64-unknown-none.\n- Fiber keeps its behaviour; the per-arch first-frame layout is shared so both layers stay in step.\n\nConsumer: Zura component scheduler (zura 9fd1c4b).","files":null}]}